    {
      "id": "k2cjlwe",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "1. Factors Affecting the Climate of India"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "India's climate is a Tropical Monsoon Climate primarily governed by the seasonal reversal of winds.",
        "The monsoon originates from differential heating between the Indian landmass and oceans, aided by ITCZ shifts and Tibetan Plateau heating.",
        "The Southwest Monsoon splits into two branches upon striking the Indian peninsula due to topography.",
        "Rainfall occurs through orographic uplift, convection, and cyclonic activity, but distribution remains highly uneven.",
        "El Niño causes abnormal warming of eastern Pacific waters and weakens the monsoon, whereas La Niña strengthens it."
      ],
      "id": "xvw59on"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "India’s climate is influenced by its latitudinal position, relief, altitude, distance from sea, pressure systems and jet streams. These factors operate together to produce extreme regional diversity in temperature and rainfall.",
      "id": "9am1bqm"
    },
    {
